Dockerfile and Docker Build

Overview

A Dockerfile is a text file that contains instructions for building a Docker Image. During my internship learning, I studied the basic structure of a Dockerfile and how an image can be built from it.

1. What is a Dockerfile?

A Dockerfile is a file containing a set of instructions that Docker uses to create an image.

It helps define:

- Base image
- Application files
- Required dependencies
- Working directory
- Commands
- Application startup instructions

2. Basic Dockerfile Structure

A simple Dockerfile can contain instructions such as:

FROM
WORKDIR
COPY
RUN
CMD
EXPOSE

3. FROM

The FROM instruction specifies the base image used to build the new image.

Example:

FROM node:18

This means that the Node.js 18 image is used as the base image.

4. WORKDIR

The WORKDIR instruction sets the working directory inside the container.

Example:

WORKDIR /app

After this instruction, commands are executed from the `/app` directory.

5. COPY

The COPY instruction copies files or directories from the local system into the Docker image.

Example:

COPY . .

This copies the application files into the current working directory of the image.

6. RUN

The RUN instruction executes a command while the Docker image is being built.

Example:

RUN npm install

This can be used to install application dependencies during the image-building process.

7. CMD

The CMD instruction specifies the default command that runs when a container is started from the image.

Example:

CMD ["npm", "start"]

8. EXPOSE

The EXPOSE instruction indicates the port on which the application inside the container is expected to listen.

Example:

EXPOSE 3000

It is mainly used to document the intended application port.

9. Example Dockerfile

A basic example of a Dockerfile is:

FROM node:18

WORKDIR /app

COPY package*.json ./

RUN npm install

COPY . .

EXPOSE 3000

CMD ["npm", "start"]

10. Building a Docker Image

After creating a Dockerfile, a Docker image can be built using:

docker build -t my-app .

Here:

- `docker build` builds an image.
- `-t` assigns a name to the image.
- `my-app` is the image name.
- `.` specifies the current directory as the build context.

11. Checking the Image

After building the image, it can be checked using:

docker images

This displays the Docker images available on the system.

12. Running the Built Image

The image can be used to create a container using:

docker run my-app

If the application uses a specific port, port mapping can also be used.

Example:

docker run -p 3000:3000 my-app

13. Basic Dockerfile Workflow

The basic workflow is:

Create Dockerfile
       ↓
Write Dockerfile instructions
       ↓
docker build
       ↓
Docker Image
       ↓
docker run
       ↓
Docker Container

14. Learning Outcome

Through this topic, I learned the purpose and basic structure of a Dockerfile and understood how Dockerfile instructions are used to build a Docker image.

I also learned the basic process of building an image and running a container from that image.
